# Testing

This document is the repository-specific authority for test commands, feedback tiers, lane ownership, and expected runtime. Keep concrete setup and measurements here rather than relying on a shared convention that cannot reflect the repository's language, dependencies, or execution environment.

<!-- awf:include durable-oracle -->

<!-- awf:section gate -->
## Gate

Use the narrowest relevant test while editing, then run the repository's required gate at its documented boundary. A red required gate blocks progress: fix the cause or revert rather than weakening the check. Prefer hooks or CI for repeated gate invocation, and do not manually duplicate a gate already owned by that automation.

Document every feedback command with its trigger, owner, included and excluded checks, failure behavior, and environment requirements. Keep network access, external services, generated runtimes, credentials, platform constraints, and shared mutable state explicit.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section tiers -->
## Tiers and lanes

Separate feedback by purpose rather than calling every check a gate.

| Feedback class | Trigger | Intended scope |
|---|---|---|
| Focused | During editing | The narrowest test, build, or lint command that exercises the changed owner. |
| Affected | After a coherent edit when supported | Changed owners, dependents, test importers, and declared smoke packages selected from explicit change evidence. |
| Fast or normal gate | Commit boundary | Deterministic, bounded checks that provide prompt commit feedback. |
| Terminal or hosted | Implementation completion, push, or CI | Required repository-wide, runtime, or platform assurance omitted from earlier feedback. |

The repository decides the exact commands and lane membership. A fast tier may optimize turnaround, but it must not imply exhaustive behavioral assurance when tests run elsewhere. Put generated-runtime and cross-language checks in a consistently enforced tier, never an undocumented manual exception.

### Duration and performance

Record representative warm and cold durations when command cost affects workflow. Bind each measurement to the command, toolchain, operating system and architecture, relevant hardware, cache state, concurrency, and sample method. Name dominant stages so a regression can be located instead of treating total wall time as one opaque value.

Durations are evidence, not universal correctness assertions. Use repository-specific targets or budgets only when their measurement procedure is reproducible. A slow required lane still needs an explicit trigger and owner; move it to a deliberate hosted tier rather than silently skipping it. Document serial-only tests, isolated homes or temporary directories, cache-sensitive behavior, and external dependencies that materially affect timing or reliability.
<!-- awf:end -->

